<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cortes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> were the representative assemblies of the Iberian kingdoms, in which the king met the clergy, nobility and towns to grant taxes, hear grievances and swear to uphold the laws. That of León in 1188 is among the earliest European parliaments to include town representatives; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon, Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Portugal had their own. The name passed to the modern parliaments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Called back to Lisbon by the Liberal Revolution of 1820, John VI left his heir, who refused the <b>Cortes</b>' demand to reduce Brazil to a colony on the Dia do Fico, 9 January 1822, and was crowned Emperor Pedro I after independence on 7 September 1822.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…were anointed by the Church and claimed to rule by the grace of God, but they shared power with great nobles, with assemblies such as the English Parliament and the <b>Cortes</b> of the Iberian kingdoms, and with a Church that could excommunicate them and, popes claimed, depose them.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Althing of Iceland met from about 930, Alfonso IX of León summoned townsmen to his <b>Cortes</b> in 1188, and in England Simon de Montfort’s parliament of 1265 included representatives of the towns; the word comes from the French parler, “to speak”.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Portuguese Cortes</b> sought to reduce Brazil's status, Pedro declared independence on 7 September 1822 and founded the Empire of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cortes</b> chose Amadeo of Savoy, but Prim was assassinated in December 1870, days before he arrived.</blockquote><a href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a></li><li><blockquote>On 3 January 1874 General Manuel Pavía dissolved the <b>Cortes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-first-republic">Spanish First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
